W_{0,p}(s,phi) for a p-SCALED lattice, with its normalisation CALIBRATED.

Write, for rank n = 2,

    alpha_k(phi) = p^{-k(n-1)} #{x in (mu+L)/p^k L : Q(x) = 0 mod p^k},
    G(X)         = sum_{k>=0} alpha_k X^k,            X = p^{-s}.

CLAIM:      W_{0,p}(s,phi) = (1 - X) * G(X).

The normalisation is not guessed.  It is pinned by two anchors from Schofer's
own Lemma 2.18, which computes prod_p W_{0,p}(s,phi_0) = c_0 L(s,chi)/L(s+1,chi)
in the unimodular case, so the local factor there must be the p-factor of that
ratio:

  (a) unimodular SPLIT plane (Q = xy, chi_d(p) = +1)   ->  (1 - X/p)/(1 - X)
  (b) unimodular INERT plane (Q = x^2 - u y^2, = -1)   ->  (1 + X/p)/(1 + X)

and it is then CONFIRMED against an object computed by completely different
code:

  (c) the p-scaled split plane at a nonzero isotropic mu  ->  1,
      which is exactly what Magma's LocalWhittakerPolynomial returns (km0poly.m).

The object we actually need is the fourth:

  (d) the p-scaled split plane at mu = 0 -- the configuration a firing
      discriminant forces at the level prime, and the one the Magma path
      cannot return because the series does not terminate.
      Answer:  (1 + (p-2)X)/(1 - X),  which has a simple POLE at X = 1.

That pole is the whole story: see the derivation printed at the end.

==============================================================================

p = 3  (u = 2, a nonsquare)
  (a) unimodular split      alpha_k = 1, 5/3, 7/3, 3, 11/3
      W(X) = (1 - 1/3X) / (1 - 1X)    reproduces alpha_k: YES
  (b) unimodular inert      alpha_k = 1, 1/3, 1, 1/3, 1
      W(X) = (1 + 1/3X) / (1 + 1X)    reproduces alpha_k: YES
  (c) p-scaled split mu!=0  alpha_k = 1, 1, 1, 1, 1
      W(X) = (1) / (1)    reproduces alpha_k: YES
  (d) p-scaled split mu=0   alpha_k = 1, 3, 5, 7, 9
      W(X) = (1 + 1X) / (1 - 1X)    reproduces alpha_k: YES

p = 5  (u = 2, a nonsquare)
  (a) unimodular split      alpha_k = 1, 9/5, 13/5, 17/5
      W(X) = (1 - 1/5X) / (1 - 1X)    reproduces alpha_k: YES
  (b) unimodular inert      alpha_k = 1, 1/5, 1, 1/5
      W(X) = (1 + 1/5X) / (1 + 1X)    reproduces alpha_k: YES
  (c) p-scaled split mu!=0  alpha_k = 1, 1, 1, 1
      W(X) = (1) / (1)    reproduces alpha_k: YES
  (d) p-scaled split mu=0   alpha_k = 1, 5, 9, 13
      W(X) = (1 + 3X) / (1 - 1X)    reproduces alpha_k: YES

p = 7  (u = 3, a nonsquare)
  (a) unimodular split      alpha_k = 1, 13/7, 19/7, 25/7
      W(X) = (1 - 1/7X) / (1 - 1X)    reproduces alpha_k: YES
  (b) unimodular inert      alpha_k = 1, 1/7, 1, 1/7
      W(X) = (1 + 1/7X) / (1 + 1X)    reproduces alpha_k: YES
  (c) p-scaled split mu!=0  alpha_k = 1, 1, 1, 1
      W(X) = (1) / (1)    reproduces alpha_k: YES
  (d) p-scaled split mu=0   alpha_k = 1, 7, 13, 19
      W(X) = (1 + 5X) / (1 - 1X)    reproduces alpha_k: YES

==============================================================================
CALIBRATION VERDICT: all four families match

Anchors (a),(b) are Schofer's own L-ratio, so the normalisation W = (1-X)G(X)
is his.  (c) is returned independently by Magma's LocalWhittakerPolynomial as
the constant 1, and comes out of this normalisation too.  So (d) is entitled
to the same normalisation, and it is

        W_{0,N}(s, phi_0)  =  (1 + (N-2)X) / (1 - X),      X = N^{-s},

with a SIMPLE POLE at s = 0:  1 - X = s log N + O(s^2), numerator -> N-1, so

        W_{0,N}(s, phi_0)  =  (N-1)/(s log N)  +  O(1).

DERIVATION OF kappa_mu(0).

km0places.m: a nonzero isotropic mu is supported at the LEVEL PRIME ALONE, and
km0poly.m: W_{0,N}(s,phi_mu) = 1 there.  Every other local factor is therefore
the SAME for phi_mu and for phi_0, so with P(s) := prod_p W_{0,p}(s,phi_0),

        prod_p W_{0,p}(s, phi_mu)  =  P(s) / W_{0,N}(s, phi_0).

The individual factors at s=0 are poles -- only the product converges -- so this
ratio, not any single place, is the right object.  Schofer's Lemma 2.18 gives

        E_0(tau,s;phi,+1) = v^{s/2} phi(0) - A(s) prod_p W_{0,p}(s,phi_p),
        A(s) = sqrt(pi) i v^{-s/2} Gamma((s+1)/2)/Gamma(s/2+1),   A(0) = i*pi.

At phi_0 the coefficient vanishes at s=0 (functional equation), which PINS

        1 = A(0) P(0).

At mu != 0 the first term is absent, and P(s)/W_{0,N}(s,phi_0) has a simple
ZERO because W_{0,N}(s,phi_0) has a simple POLE:

        prod_p W_{0,p}(s,phi_mu) = P(0) * s log N/(N-1) + O(s^2).

Hence, with A(0)P(0) = 1 killing every transcendental constant,

        kappa_mu(0) = b_{phi_mu}(0) = -A(0) P(0) log N/(N-1) = - log N / (N-1).

So the m=0 term at a nonzero isotropic coset is an s-DERIVATIVE after all --
the paper's section 10 reading is right -- but the log N comes from the level
prime, not from a Diff place: not because its factor VANISHES, but because at
phi_0 it has a POLE, and replacing it by 1 turns that pole into a zero.

ASSEMBLY.  There are 2N-2 nonzero isotropic cosets, all carrying the same
c_eta(0).  In the normalisation of the paper's section 1,

    sum_tau log|Psi_F| = -(|CM(d)|/4) sum_eta sum_m c_eta(-m) kappa_eta(m),

the dropped m=0 terms contribute, per CM point,

    -(1/4) * (2N-2) * c_eta(0) * ( - log N/(N-1) )  =  + (1/2) c_eta(0) log N.

The measured correction is  mult(f) * log N  with  mult(f) = (1/2) c_eta(0).

    ==>  MATCH, and the 1/2 is (1/4)*(2N-2)/(N-1) = 1/2, INDEPENDENT OF N,
         which is why the same 1/2 was measured at N = 2, 3 and 5.

